textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-metafisica":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: metaphysics is very abstract, so read each section with its question in mind, underline the <strong>terms in bold</strong> (they are the vocabulary of the exam) and check that you can explain each position with an example of your own. The tables are for revision. In metaphysics almost nothing is settled: you are asked to understand each position and its main objection well, not to choose the ‘right’ one.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If we compare metaphysics with other forms of knowledge, five distinctive features appear. It is an <strong>orientating</strong> form of knowledge: what we answer about what the real is, whether we are free or whether everything is matter ends up influencing the meaning we give to life. It is <strong>total</strong>: biology studies living things and economics studies exchanges, but metaphysics does not carve out a portion; rather, it asks about the whole. It is <strong>universal</strong>: Parmenides, after discarding the path of non-being, says that only one account of the path remains, ‘that it is’ (fragment B8, 1-2). Everything there is, by the mere fact of being, falls under that question, whether an electron, a memory or a number. It is <strong>open and unfinished</strong>: its questions are not closed like a maths exercise, because each answer opens others. That is why we continue to discuss what the Greeks discussed. And it depends on <strong>its own definitions</strong>: each philosopher defines metaphysics from within their system, so that saying what it is already means taking sides. That is why it is advisable to speak of <em>metaphysics</em>, in the plural.</p><p>An example. An Aristotelian would define it as the science that studies being as being and seeks the first principles and causes of everything (Aristotle, <em>Metaphysics</em>, IV, 1). A Kantian would begin with another question: can reason know anything beyond experience? It is not for nothing that Kant entitled a work <em>Prolegomena to Any Future Metaphysics That Will Be Able to Present Itself as a Science</em> (1783). For the former, metaphysics describes reality. For the latter, before describing it, one must examine the limits of the one who describes it.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> can a form of knowledge be at once universal, because it encompasses everything, and unfinished, because it never ends? Or does it not end precisely because it encompasses everything?</p>\n<h2>1. What is metaphysics? (and why science does not replace it)</h2>\n<p>Does Olentzero exist? Every December he fills streets, songs and the letters of thousands of children, and even so nobody would say that he is real in the way that any pebble on the bed of the Bay of Biscay is real, which nobody has seen or ever will see and which matters to nobody. What matters most to us does not always exist, and what exists does not always matter to us. So what does it mean to <strong>exist</strong>? And what separates what is real from what lives only in our stories? This is the kind of question with which <strong>metaphysics</strong> begins.</p>\n<p>The same question, with other protagonists, is a dilemma to decide: in <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-existir\">‘The village and the superhero’</a> you choose between two answers and compare yours with those of several philosophers.</p>\n<p>Two more cases put the word ‘real’ to the test. First: you remember very clearly telling your friend Irati that you were leaving the handball team; she swears you did not, and in the end you realise that you dreamt that conversation a few weeks ago. Was it real? The dream really happened, as an event in your mind, and it left a memory as vivid as any other; what did not happen is what the dream was about. ‘Real’ is said in at least two ways: of an experience, which exists while you are having it, and of what the experience points to, which may not exist. And since the memory of something dreamt and the memory of something lived feel the same, looking ‘inwards’ is not enough to tell them apart: they have to be checked against other things, as you saw when you studied memory in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>.</p>\n<p>Second: after a match at Ipurua, three friends describe the controversial incident in three ways: one saw a blatant penalty; another, that the striker dived; the third, that it all began with an earlier foul that nobody blew the whistle for. Cinema has a famous film about this, <em>Rashōmon</em> (Akira Kurosawa, 1950), in which four witnesses tell the story of the same crime in incompatible ways. What is interesting is what we take for granted when we argue: that there was <strong>a single incident</strong>, and that each version is right or wrong depending on whether it matches it. If they were all equally real, there would be no point in watching the replay. That assumption, that there is something that is the way it is even if we tell it badly, is the idea of a reality independent of us; you will see who questions it and why in section 2 and in the relativism of Topic 3.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if a dream can leave a memory identical to that of something you lived through, what assures you that what you remember of this morning really happened? And is it enough for all the witnesses to agree for something to be real, or can they all be wrong at once?</p>\n<p>The name comes, according to the most widespread explanation, from the arrangement of the works of <strong>Aristotle</strong>: some books on being were placed after those on physics, <em>ta meta ta physika</em>, ‘those that come after physics’. Aristotle spoke of ‘first philosophy’, the study of <strong>being as being</strong>: of what it is to exist. Today metaphysics asks what there really is, what it is made of, what time and change are, whether we are free or whether God exists. The part that studies what kinds of things exist is called <strong>ontology</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Being is said in many ways</h3><p>Look at these sentences: “Nerea is from Tolosa”, “the exam is on Thursday”, “seven is a prime number”, “there is a problem with the wi-fi”. In all of them, the verb <strong>to be</strong> or the idea that something is appears in one form or another, but it does not mean the same. Sometimes it says what something is; at other times, where or when it is, or simply that it exists. Aristotle summed this up in a formula: “that which is is said in many ways”, <em>tò òn légetai pollakhôs</em> (<em>Metaphysics</em>, IV, 2, 1003a33). But he added something decisive: these ways are not a chaos. All of them refer to a central sense, substance, which you saw in section 4: colour, size or place are “being” because they are of a substance.</p><p>For this reason, “being” is not <strong>univocal</strong>, like “triangle”, which means exactly the same in every case, nor <strong>equivocal</strong>, like “bank”, which names a riverbank and a financial institution with no relation between the two. It lies halfway between the two: its senses are different, but they are related. Medieval philosophers called this the <strong>analogy of being</strong>.</p><p>In 1927, Martin Heidegger opened <em>Being and Time</em> (§ 1-2) by accusing philosophy of having forgotten that question. It has concerned itself with <strong>beings</strong>, with the things that are (atoms, souls, God), and has taken for granted what it means for them to be. It is said that “being” is the most general concept, that it cannot be defined and that we all understand it; for Heidegger, that does not close the question, but rather shows that we remain in the dark. The being of beings is not itself one more being: it is what he later called the <strong>ontological difference</strong>. You can describe all the parts of your phone without having yet said what it means for it to exist.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is “existing” just one more property of things, like colour or weight, or is it something of another kind? If no one can define it, how is it that we all use it without being mistaken?</p>\n<p>A researcher measures how long it takes a culture of bacteria to grow. She takes it for granted that the bacteria are still there even when nobody is looking at them, that their growth has causes and that what she observes today will hold tomorrow. These assumptions do not come out of the experiment: they make it possible. Examining them is the task of metaphysics, which does not compete with science: it <strong>asks about what science assumes</strong>. And the border moves: whether the universe had a beginning was for centuries a purely metaphysical question (Aristotle believed it was eternal); with <strong>Lemaître</strong>’s Big Bang theory, cosmology was able to study it, though without deciding whether it was an absolute beginning.</p>\n<p>In the eighteenth century, the German Christian Wolff (1679-1754) gave metaphysics the structure that was taught for decades in universities. Wolff distinguished two parts. <strong>General metaphysics</strong> or <strong>ontology</strong> studies being as being, that is, what holds for anything that exists: possibility, essence, cause. <strong>Special metaphysics</strong> is divided into three branches according to its object: <strong>rational psychology</strong>, which studies the soul; <strong>rational cosmology</strong>, which studies the world as a whole; and <strong>rational or natural theology</strong>, which studies God. He devoted a Latin treatise to each part: <em>Philosophia prima sive ontologia</em> (1730), <em>Cosmologia generalis</em> (1731), <em>Psychologia rationalis</em> (1734) and <em>Theologia naturalis</em> (1736-1737). ‘Rational’ here means that these objects are intended to be known by reason alone, without relying on experience or revelation.</p><p>Each branch has its questions. Rational psychology asks whether consciousness survives the death of the brain. Rational cosmology, whether the universe had a beginning or has always existed. Rational theology, whether there is a necessary being on which everything that might not exist depends.</p><p>Kant knew this scheme well, because he taught it for years in his lectures using a manual from the Wolffian school, Baumgarten’s <em>Metaphysica</em>. In the Transcendental Dialectic of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> he goes through it branch by branch to show that reason, when it seeks to know the soul, the world as a totality and God, falls into unavoidable illusions. About the soul it commits false inferences, the paralogisms. About the world it contradicts itself in the antinomies; the first is precisely whether the world had a beginning in time. About God it offers proofs that do not achieve their aim. The questions are natural, but they do not yield knowledge.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> which branch is still most alive today? Think about neuroscience and Big Bang cosmology: have any of those questions passed into the hands of science, and have any been left without an owner?</p>\n<p>It has also had its detractors. In 1932 <strong>Rudolf Carnap</strong>, of the <strong>Vienna Circle</strong>, quoted Heidegger’s sentence ‘the nothing itself nothings’: no observation confirms it and it is not a logical truth, so it is meaningless, like all metaphysics. But that criterion, ‘only what can be checked by experience has meaning’, cannot itself be checked by experience. And what a laboratory cannot decide is not absurd for that reason: ‘humiliating people for fun is wrong’ is perfectly understandable. Earlier, <strong>Kant</strong> (<em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, 1781) had set a more moderate limit: we know only what can be given in experience; of the soul, of the world as a totality or of God there is no scientific knowledge.</p>\n<h3>Three suspicions against metaphysics, and a response</h3><p>Besides Carnap’s critique, there were others that did not ask whether metaphysics makes sense, but whom it serves. Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, in <em>The German Ideology</em> (written in 1845-1846 and published much later, in 1932), maintained that the dominant ideas of each age are those of the class that dominates the economy. A metaphysics that presents the social order as eternal or willed by God would be <strong>ideology</strong>: a thought that disguises interests as truths.</p><p>Friedrich Nietzsche, in <em>Twilight of the Idols</em> (written in 1888, published in 1889), recounted in six steps ‘How the “true world” finally became a fable’: from Plato’s Ideas to the Christian afterlife and Kant’s thing in itself, that higher world gradually becomes unattainable, then useless and finally dispensable. And, once it is abolished, the idea that this is an ‘apparent’ world also disappears. For Nietzsche, inventing another world was a way of despising life.</p><p>Michel Foucault, in works such as <em>Discipline and Punish</em> (1975), showed that knowledge is tied to <strong>power</strong>: deciding what is normal, healthy or sane serves to classify and correct people. The great truths about ‘human nature’ would also be instruments of control.</p><p>Anyone who defends metaphysics replies that these criticisms do not escape it. To say that everything depends on the economy, that there is no world other than this one, or that all truth is an effect of power is to advance theses about what there is and how it ultimately is. Moreover, discovering where an idea comes from does not prove that it is false. Their critics answer that the value of suspicion lies not in replacing one metaphysics with another, but in asking whom each theory benefits.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can one criticise all metaphysics without doing metaphysics? And if an idea benefits those who defend it, is it any less true for that?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: metaphysics asks what exists and what it is ultimately like; it neither replaces science nor is replaced by it, because it examines the assumptions from which every science starts.</strong></p>\n<h2>2. Appearance and reality: is the world what it seems?</h2>\n<p>The first great metaphysical question arises from a suspicion: that things are not as they seem. In Topic 3 we raised it from the standpoint of knowledge (can we trust our senses?); here the question is <strong>what is real</strong>, regardless of how we know it.</p>\n<p><strong>Parmenides of Elea</strong> (6th–5th centuries BC) took the suspicion to the extreme. He reasoned thus: what is, is, and what is not, is not; non-being cannot even be thought. But changing means passing from not being something to being it, and moving means occupying an empty place, a ‘non-being’. Therefore <strong>being</strong> is one, eternal, motionless and unchanging, and the change and multiplicity we see are mere <strong>appearance</strong>. For him, if reason and senses clash, reason rules.</p>\n<p>The word ‘reality’ comes from the Latin <em>realitas</em>, derived from <em>res</em>, ‘thing’. Its philosophical use spread in Scholasticism, and John Duns Scotus, at the turn of the thirteenth to the fourteenth century, contributed greatly to this. For Scotus, within one and the same thing there can be several ‘realities’ that are distinguished without being separate things. In a person, for example, being an animal and being rational are distinguished in this way: this is the so-called formal distinction. And what makes this individual precisely this one, and not another equal one, is also a positive reality, which his followers called <em>haecceitas</em>, ‘thisness’.</p><p>In the twentieth century, two Spanish philosophers made reality the centre of their thought. Ortega y Gasset (<em>What Is Philosophy?</em>, 1929 lecture course) seeks the most basic reality, the one that does not depend on any other, and finds it in ‘my life’. My life is not my body or my mind, but the encounter of the self with its circumstance: what I do and what happens to me. That is why he calls it <strong>radical reality</strong>: any other reality, whether an atom or God, becomes present to me within it. Xavier Zubiri (<em>On Essence</em>, 1962; <em>Sentient Intelligence</em>, 1980-1983) took another step. For him, something is real when what it has it has <strong>‘of its own’</strong>, and not only insofar as it appears to me.</p><p>Think of a stone from the bottom of the Ullíbarri reservoir. It weighs, is hard and has a chemical composition even though no one has ever seen it. Those properties are its own, not lent by whoever looks at it. For that reason, for Zubiri, the stone is real. For Ortega, by contrast, that stone too only counts for me when it appears in my life, even if as something I have not seen.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> is your own life the safest starting point for thinking about the real, or does it risk leaving out everything that exists without you?</p>\n<p>The fragments of Parmenides’ poem, in our own translation from the Greek and with a reading guide, are in <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-parmenides-camino\">‘Parmenides: the path of what is’</a>.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-11\"><strong>Plato</strong> (427–347 BC) resolved it in another way: there are two levels of reality. The <strong>sensible world</strong>, that of the things we see and touch, changes ceaselessly and is an imperfect copy. The <strong>intelligible world</strong>, or world of <strong>Forms</strong>, contains the eternal and unchanging models of things: Beauty, Justice, Equality. In the <strong>allegory of the cave</strong> (<em>The Republic</em>, Book VII), prisoners chained since childhood see only shadows on a wall and take them for what is real; one of them, freed, goes outside and discovers the light of the sun. For Plato, the Forms are <strong>more real</strong> than sensible things.</p>\n<p>The question is still alive. In <em>The Matrix</em> (1999), humanity lives connected to a simulation that it takes for the real world. <strong>Nick Bostrom</strong> proposed in 2003 the <strong>simulation argument</strong>: if any civilisation comes to simulate conscious minds on a large scale, there could be many more simulated minds than ‘original’ ones, and we would have no good reasons to be sure we are not one of them. Bostrom does not claim that we live in a simulation, only that this is one of several possibilities. If everything were simulated, would your life be any less real?</p>\n<p>To decide how you would live with a doubt like that, the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-cubeta\">‘The brain in a vat’</a>; to go over the section, from Plato’s cave to simulation, the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-real\">‘What is real?’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: since Parmenides and Plato, metaphysics has distinguished between what seems and what really is; appearance is not ‘nothing’, but neither does it have the last word.</strong></p>\n<h2>3. What is everything made of? Monism and pluralism; idealism and materialism</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-15\">The first Greek philosophers sought the <strong>first principle</strong> (<em>arkhé</em>) of everything: water for Thales, air for Anaximenes. Empedocles proposed four elements and Democritus, infinite <strong>atoms</strong> in the void. The question is: how many basic types of reality, or <strong>substance</strong>, are there, and of what kind?</p>\n<p>Faced with the real, there are two very different questions. Imagine the Otzarreta beech forest. You can ask <em>what it is</em>: beeches of a certain age, a mossy ground, a particular humidity. That question is answered by looking, measuring and describing. But you can also ask <em>why there is</em> forest, and ultimately why there is anything. That second question does not seek more data, but a foundation.</p><p>From this arise two ways of understanding reality. For the first, the real is a <strong>foundation</strong> that gives a reason for the world. Aristotle ends his <em>Metaphysics</em> (Book XII) with a prime unmoved mover on which all movement depends. Thomas Aquinas follows his ways to a first cause. Leibniz formulates the <strong>principle of sufficient reason</strong>: nothing exists or occurs without a reason why it is thus and not otherwise (<em>Monadology</em>, 32). And from that principle he derives the question ‘why is there something rather than nothing?’ (<em>Principles of Nature and Grace</em>, 7). Those who follow this path usually end up with God as the ultimate reason. For the second way, of empiricist root, the real is what is <strong>concrete and perceptible</strong>: this beech, this rain. We explain some things by others, but without leaving the world.</p><p>Whoever does not seek a foundation accepts that reality may not be explained completely. Hume argues this in his <em>Dialogues concerning Natural Religion</em> (Part IX): if we have explained each part of a chain of causes, demanding in addition the cause of the whole is an arbitrary requirement of the mind. Russell, in the debate with Copleston that you already saw in connection with the cosmological argument, held something similar. Today there is talk of accepting the universe as a <strong>brute fact</strong>, something that simply is so.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> is it reasonable to demand an explanation for everything? And if it is not, where do you decide to stop asking ‘why’?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-16\">According to number, there are three answers. <strong>Monism</strong> holds that at bottom there is only one type of reality: <strong>Spinoza</strong> (1632–1677) defended in his <em>Ethics</em> that there is a single substance, ‘God or Nature’, of which thought and extension are two attributes. <strong>Dualism</strong> asserts that there are two: for <strong>Descartes</strong>, the thinking substance (<em>res cogitans</em>) and the extended one (<em>res extensa</em>). <strong>Pluralism</strong> admits many, like Empedocles’ four elements or Leibniz’s <strong>monads</strong>.</p>\n<p>How Spinoza’s theory of knowledge, his ethics and his politics follow from his monism you can see in the exploration <a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">‘Spinoza: philosophy as a system’</a>.</p>\n<p>According to their nature, there are two great answers. <strong>Materialism</strong> holds that everything that exists is matter or depends on it: so thought Democritus and <strong>Hobbes</strong>, and so thinks today <strong>physicalism</strong>, for which what is real is what physics describes, or could describe. <strong>Idealism</strong> asserts that reality is, ultimately, mental or spiritual. For <strong>Berkeley</strong> (1685–1753), to be is to be perceived (<em>esse est percipi</em>): a table is the set of perceptions we have of it, and if nobody looks at it, it goes on existing because God perceives it.</p>\n<p>An example: what is a song? Vibrations of the air, impulses in your brain, data in a file… or the experience of listening to it? The materialist will say that everything is organised matter; the idealist, that we only directly know experiences; the dualist, that there are two distinct realities that have to be related.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Thesis</th><th>Authors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Materialist monism</strong></td><td>There is only matter; the mental depends on it</td><td>Democritus, Hobbes, present-day physicalism</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Idealist monism</strong></td><td>There is only mind or spirit; things are perceptions</td><td>Berkeley</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Spinoza’s monism</strong></td><td>A single substance with two known attributes: thought and extension</td><td>Spinoza</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dualism</strong></td><td>Two substances: the thinking one and the extended one</td><td>Descartes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pluralism</strong></td><td>Many principles or substances</td><td>Empedocles, Leibniz</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Two more positions: neutral monism and constructivism</h3>\n<p>What if matter and mind were two ways of ordering one and the same thing that is neither one nor the other? This is <strong>neutral monism</strong>. <a href=\"#biografias/james\">William James</a> put it forward in ‘Does “Consciousness” Exist?’ (1904): what is basic would be <strong>pure experience</strong>, and one and the same portion of it counts twice, depending on what we relate it to. His example is the room in which one is reading: it is part of the history of the house, with its building work and its owners, and at the same time part of the history of the person who sees it, with what they feel on entering. The same goes for the sound of a txalaparta at a festival: in one series it is the vibration of the wood and the air, the one physics studies; in another, what you heard that night. They are not two things, but one thing ordered in two ways. <a href=\"#biografias/russell\">Bertrand Russell</a> adopted it in <em>The Analysis of Mind</em> (1921): the stuff of the world would be ‘neither mind nor matter, but something more primitive than either’.</p>\n<p>It has a family resemblance to Spinoza’s monism, for whom thought and extension are two attributes of a single substance (you can see this in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">‘Spinoza: philosophy as a system’</a>). Its difficulty is saying what that neutral stuff is: if it is described in the words of physics, it looks like materialism; if in the words of experience, like idealism. Even so, it has returned to the current debate: some philosophers of mind, among them <a href=\"#biografias/chalmers\">Chalmers</a>, are exploring what is called <strong>Russellian monism</strong> as a possible way out of the hard problem of consciousness, which you will see in section 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Metaphysical constructivism</strong> goes further than the kind you saw in Topic 3. There it was a thesis about <strong>knowledge</strong> and truth; here, about <strong>what there is</strong>: objects themselves, and not just our ideas about them, would depend on our concepts. For money, or the border between Irun and Hendaye, almost everyone accepts this: these are Searle’s <strong>institutional facts</strong>, which exist because we recognise them, whereas the Bidasoa would flow just the same without any agreement (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>). The question is whether all of reality is like that.</p>\n<p>The strong version answers yes. For <strong>Nelson Goodman</strong> (<em>Ways of Worldmaking</em>, 1978) there is no world already carved up for our descriptions to copy: we make the stars as we make the constellations, by drawing their boundaries. <strong>Hilary Putnam</strong> (<em>The Many Faces of Realism</em>, 1987) proposed an example: how many objects are there in a world with three individuals? Three, says common sense; seven, if all the sums of individuals (each pair and the trio) also count as objects. According to Putnam, the answer depends on the conceptual scheme we choose, and none of them is ‘the world’s own’. The philosopher Israel Scheffler objected to Goodman (1980) that the stars were shining long before anyone carved them out: did we make them backwards in time? Goodman replied that what we make is a true version in which there are stars that predate us. The debate remains open.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: would La Liga exist if nobody recognised it? And Mount Anboto? Where would you put the boundary between what we construct and what we find, and which side does a piece for txalaparta like the one in this section’s example fall on?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: monism, dualism and pluralism answer how many basic realities there are; materialism and idealism, what type of reality is the fundamental one.</strong></p>\n<p>To speak about anything we need certain very general concepts: to say how much it is, what it is like, where it is or what it relates to. These concepts, with which we know, order and express reality, are called <strong>categories</strong>. Aristotle listed ten (<em>Categories</em>, 4): substance, quantity, quality, relation, place, time, position, possession (or ‘having’), action and passion. For him they are the supreme genera of what there is, the basic ways in which something can be. Kant gave them a different meaning in the Transcendental Analytic of the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>. There he proposes twelve categories, grouped into quantity, quality, relation and modality, and understands them as <strong>concepts of the understanding</strong>. We do not read them in things: we impose them ourselves so that experience has order. Causality is one of them.</p><p>It is worth distinguishing two levels. There are categories <strong>proper to each field of knowledge</strong>: physics speaks of ‘field’, biology of ‘species’, art criticism of ‘style’ or ‘genre’. And there are <strong>universal</strong> categories, which apply to anything: quantity, quality, matter, relation.</p><p>Try it with your mobile. With universal categories it is a substance, this concrete device; it has a quantity, about 180 grams; a quality, black and somewhat scratched; a relation, newer than your brother’s; a place, the rucksack; and a time, bought last summer. With the categories of technology you would speak of processor, memory, battery capacity in mAh, operating system or 5G connection. The latter only apply to devices; the former also apply to a stone or a song.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> are categories from reality, as Aristotle thought, or from our mind, as Kant thought? If they were only ours, why do they work so well with the world?</p>\n<h2>4. Substance, essence and existence</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-22\"><strong>Aristotle</strong> (384–322 BC), a pupil of Plato, rejected the idea that the Forms exist in a separate world. What is real is above all concrete things: this horse, this tree, Socrates. He called each one a <strong>substance</strong> (<em>ousía</em>): what exists in itself and supports its qualities. Qualities are <strong>accidents</strong>: colour, size or place exist only in a substance.</p>\n<p>Each physical substance is composed of <strong>matter</strong>, that of which it is made, and of <strong>form</strong>, that which makes it what it is: in a statue, the bronze acts as matter and the figure as form. This is <strong>hylomorphism</strong>. In broad terms, form corresponds to <strong>essence</strong>, that which answers the question ‘what is it?’. A horse can vary in colour and still be a horse; if it loses its essence, however, it ceases to be one.</p>\n<p>With these concepts Aristotle answered Parmenides. One thing can be something <strong>in actuality</strong> (what it already is) and another thing <strong>in potentiality</strong> (what it can come to be): the acorn is an oak in potentiality, and someone who is starting to learn the guitar is a guitarist in potentiality. Changing is not passing from nothing to being, but making a potentiality real.</p>\n<h3>The final cause: what for?</h3><p>There is a question we ask all day long: what for? It climbs a ladder. First, <strong>acts</strong>: you go to the park so that the dog can run; the end is in the head of the person who acts. Second, <strong>artefacts</strong>: the pencil is for writing; the end was put there by whoever made it, even though the pencil knows nothing. Third, <strong>the functions of living beings</strong>: we eat and sleep in order to live; here there is no longer anyone who has decided anything, and yet the question seems to make sense. Fourth, the <strong>seed</strong> that grows to become a tree: it seems that the final result guides the whole process. Aristotle called <strong>final cause</strong> that ‘that for the sake of which’ something is or happens. The question that opens up is how far the ladder climbs with sense: only up to acts and artefacts, or also up to nature with no one who planned it?</p>\n<p>Aristotle distinguished <strong>four causes</strong>, or four ways of answering ‘why does this exist?’. Think of a trainera. The cause <strong>Material</strong> is that of which it is made: the wood, the resin. The cause <strong>Formal</strong> is what makes it a trainera and not a log: its design, its narrow, long hull. The cause <strong>Efficient</strong> is whoever or whatever produces the change: the shipwright with his tools. The cause <strong>Final</strong> is that for the sake of which it exists: competing in the regatta, with thirteen rowers aboard. If one is missing, the explanation is lame.</p><p>The bold thing is that Aristotle applies the final cause also to nature:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>‘The swallow makes its nest in this way, and the spider its web; plants put out leaves to protect the fruit and direct their roots downwards, not upwards, to feed themselves.’ — Aristotle, <em>Physics</em>, II, 8, 199a20-30 (own version, summarised)</p></blockquote><p>Animals act without deliberating, and yet there seems to be an end. He himself raises the objection: it rains so that the wheat grows, or it simply rains and the wheat grows; and if it rots on the threshing floor, we will not say that it rained to spoil it (198b). He replied that what is constant and regular is not the work of chance.</p>\n<p>Modern science looked with suspicion on that ‘what for’. Francis Bacon wrote in <em>De augmentis scientiarum</em> (III, 5, 1623) that the search for final causes is sterile and, like a virgin consecrated to God, begets nothing. Descartes maintained that we cannot know God's ends, and therefore discards them in physics (<em>Meditations</em> IV; <em>Principles</em> I, 28). Spinoza went further: in the appendix to Part I of the <em>Ethics</em> he says that final causes are human fictions and that this doctrine inverts nature, since it makes cause what is effect.</p><p>But biology continues to speak of functions: the heart serves to pump blood. To avoid intention, Colin Pittendrigh proposed in 1958 the term <strong>teleonomy</strong>; it was used by Jacques Monod (<em>Chance and Necessity</em>, 1970) and Ernst Mayr (1974): there are behaviours oriented towards an end by a programme formed by natural selection, without anyone planning them. On the other side, Thomas Nagel (<em>Mind and Cosmos</em>, 2012) maintains that the purely materialist explanation of the world is probably false and insists on exploring natural teleological explanations, without God.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: when you say that your eyes serve to see, are you speaking of an intention or only of what explains that the eyes are preserved? Is it the same?</p>\n<p>Centuries later, medieval philosophers such as <strong>Avicenna</strong> and <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> stressed a distinction that Aristotle had not formulated in this way: <strong>what a thing is</strong>, its <strong>essence</strong>, is not the same as the fact <strong>that it is</strong>, its <strong>existence</strong>. You can know what a dragon is without any existing. In the 20th century, <strong>Sartre</strong> inverted the relationship in the human case: ‘existence precedes essence’ (<em>Existentialism Is a Humanism</em>, 1946); first we exist and then, through what we do, we define ourselves.</p>\n<p>Aristotle distinguished the natural from the artificial by the place where the principle of its change lies (<em>Physics</em>, II, 1). An oak grows, moves and reproduces by itself. A bed has that principle outside, in the craftsman who made it. His example is clear: if you buried a bed and something sprouted, wood would come up, not another bed.</p><p>Today the boundary is blurring. The word <strong>cyborg</strong>, ‘cybernetic organism’, was proposed by Manfred Clynes and Nathan Kline in 1960, and it is no longer science fiction. A pacemaker regulates the beating of a natural heart. A cochlear implant enables hearing, and there are prostheses connected to the nervous system. The same happens in living beings. Bt maize carries a gene from the bacterium <em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em> in order to produce a protein that protects it from certain insects. Dolly the sheep, born in Scotland in 1996, was cloned from an adult cell. Dolly grew by herself, but her origin was technical: was she natural or artificial?</p><p>The most debated case is the <strong>human embryo</strong>. Knowing what it is requires ontology: is it already an individual substance, a person, or something that will become one? It also requires biology: how it develops and what changes at each stage. Only afterwards does ethics come in, and in plural societies the answers differ. For some, <strong>potentiality</strong> decides: if it is a potential person, it deserves protection from fertilisation. For others, the criterion is <strong>viability</strong>, the moment when it could live outside the womb. <strong>Gradualism</strong> holds that protection should increase with development. Each position has serious reasons, and the debate remains open.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> if someone’s organs and nerves are gradually replaced by devices, at what point does their body cease to be natural? Does the origin of a thing matter, or what it does?</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example with your mobile phone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Substance</strong></td><td>What exists in itself</td><td>This particular phone</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accident</strong></td><td>A quality that exists in a substance and can change</td><td>The case, the colour, the battery it has left</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Matter</strong></td><td>What something is made of</td><td>Glass, metal, plastic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Form / essence</strong></td><td>What makes something be what it is</td><td>Its organisation as a telephone</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Actuality / potentiality</strong></td><td>What something already is / what it can come to be</td><td>Switched off, but able to be switched on</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Existence</strong></td><td>The fact that something is, not just what it is</td><td>That it is in your backpack and not just in a catalogue</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Key idea: for Aristotle what is real are concrete substances, composed of matter and form; essence says what a thing is, and existence, that it is.</strong></p>\n<h3>Does ‘the horse’ exist? Particulars and universals</h3>\n<p>For Aristotle, what is real are concrete substances. But we constantly talk about something more: not only about this pottoka on Mount Gorbeia, but about ‘the pottoka’, ‘the horse’, ‘green’ or ‘justice’. What is individual is called a <strong>particular</strong>; what holds for many individuals, a <strong>universal</strong>. Two different pottokas share something: does that common something exist as one more thing, present in both at once? Porphyry left the question on the table around the year 270, and it became one of the great debates of the Middle Ages: the <strong>problem of universals</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are three main answers. <strong>Realism</strong> holds that universals really exist, not only in our heads. Its extreme version is Plato’s: the Idea of Horse exists apart from horses. In its moderate version, that of Aristotle and Thomas Aquinas, the common form exists, but only in things, and the understanding <strong>abstracts</strong> it from them. <strong>Conceptualism</strong> claims that universals exist only as <strong>concepts</strong> in the mind, formed from real similarities between individuals; <a href=\"#biografias/abelardo\">Peter Abelard</a> is usually placed here. <strong>Nominalism</strong> goes further: outside the mind there are only individuals, and universals are <strong>names</strong> or signs that we apply to many at once. It was defended by <a href=\"#biografias/ockham\">William of Ockham</a> (14th century), with the principle of economy we know as ‘Ockham’s razor’: entities must not be multiplied without necessity. Since for him the universal is a concept that works as a sign, some also classify him as a conceptualist.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>What is ‘horse’?</th><th>Authors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Extreme realism</strong></td><td>An Idea that exists apart from concrete horses</td><td>Plato</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Moderate realism</strong></td><td>A real form, but one that exists only in each horse; the mind abstracts it</td><td>Aristotle, Thomas Aquinas</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conceptualism</strong></td><td>A concept in the mind, based on real similarities</td><td>Abelard</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nominalism</strong></td><td>A name we apply to many individuals</td><td>Roscelin, Ockham</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The problem is not only medieval. You can group things however you like: ‘objects that fit in my rucksack’ or ‘things I was given in 2025’. But some groups seem to be drawn by nature itself: gold, water, the electron, the tiger. They are called <strong>natural kinds</strong>, and what sets them apart is that their members share many properties at once and allow us to predict: if this ring is gold, it will melt at 1,064 °C, without any need to try it. John Stuart Mill described them (<em>A System of Logic</em>, 1843), and in the 1970s Saul Kripke and Hilary Putnam added that ‘water’ names an essence that science discovers: being H<sub>2</sub>O. The realist will say that science finds kinds that were already there; the nominalist, that we classify as it suits us. In 2006 the International Astronomical Union removed Pluto from the planets: did Pluto change, or did our way of grouping?</p>\n<p>You will study the full medieval dispute in Year 2 in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-medieval\">‘Medieval philosophy: stages, methods and the problem of universals’</a>.</p>\n<h3>Things, processes or relations: three ways of understanding what is real</h3>\n<p>Beneath these discussions lies a more general question: what is most basic in reality? Three approaches can be distinguished. The first, <strong>essentialism</strong>, is the one you have just seen in Aristotle: what is basic are <strong>things</strong> (substances) with an essence that makes them what they are. A pottoka grows a thicker coat in winter and puts on weight in summer, but it is still the same pottoka, because what changes are accidents. Change is explained in terms of what remains.</p>\n<p>The second, the <strong>process</strong> approach, reverses the order: what is basic is <strong>change</strong>, and what we call ‘things’ are processes stable enough to seem still. It is the intuition of <a href=\"#biografias/heraclito\">Heraclitus</a>, whom you will see in section 6. Think of the wave that breaks on the sandbar at Mundaka: it travels hundreds of metres, but the water hardly moves; the wave is not a piece of water, but a form that travels through the water. In the 20th century <a href=\"#biografias/whitehead\">Alfred North Whitehead</a> (<em>Process and Reality</em>, 1929) built a whole metaphysics on this idea: what there is, ultimately, are <strong>events</strong> (‘actual occasions’, in his vocabulary), and a stone or a person would be long chains of them. Your body is constantly renewing many of its cells: are you a thing that changes or a process that keeps itself going?</p>\n<p>The third, the <strong>relational</strong> approach, holds that many properties, perhaps all of them, are relations. ‘Being the tallest in the class’ can stop being true without your changing, if a taller classmate arrives; the classic case is space. For <a href=\"#biografias/newton\">Newton</a> (<em>Principia</em>, 1687), space is <strong>absolute</strong>: a stage that would exist even if it were empty. For <a href=\"#biografias/leibniz\">Leibniz</a>, in his correspondence with Samuel Clarke (1715-1716), it is nothing more than the <strong>order</strong> among things that coexist: if God had placed the whole universe a metre further to the right, there would be no difference at all, so there is no separate container-space. Today the physicist <strong>Carlo Rovelli</strong> takes the idea into quantum physics with his <strong>relational interpretation</strong> (1996): the properties of a physical system are defined only in relation to another system with which it interacts, and the world would be a network of interactions rather than a collection of objects. It is a bold and much-debated proposal; the main objection to it asks whether there can be relations without anything that is related, like kinship without kin.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what is your group of friends? A thing with its essence, which stays the same even if someone joins or leaves; a process, which lasts as long as you keep getting together; or a network of relations, which disappears if they break? And what are you?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: on universals, realism places them in the Ideas or in things, conceptualism in the mind and nominalism only in names; the debate continues today with natural kinds. And what is most basic in reality can be understood as things with an essence, as processes or as relations.</strong></p>\n<h2>5. Mind and body today: can an artificial intelligence think?</h2>\n<p>In Topic 2 we saw the dualism of soul and body as a way of understanding the human being. Here the question is broader: in the inventory of what exists, are thoughts and pains something different from physical processes or are they those very processes?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-30\">Descartes’ <strong>substance dualism</strong> holds that the mind is a non-physical reality. Its great difficulty is <strong>interaction</strong>: if the mind occupies no space, how does it move the arm? Elisabeth of Bohemia raised this objection to Descartes in her letters of 1643. The <strong>identity theory</strong>, defended in the 1950s by U. T. Place and J. J. C. Smart, asserts that mental states are brain states, just as lightning is an electrical discharge. But an octopus, with a very different nervous system, also seems to feel pain: shall we say it does not feel it because it does not have our neurons?</p>\n<p>For Aristotle, substance is what exists by itself, and relations are accidents: this horse remains what it is even if it changes owner. Hegel turned this on its head. In the preface to <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em> (1807) he writes that the true is the whole. Reality is a coherent totality in which each thing is a <strong>moment</strong>, a piece linked to the others, and not an isolated block. Hence understanding something does not consist only in defining it, but also in tracing its relations until we see what place it occupies in the whole. <em>Science of Logic</em> (1812–1816) attempts to show how each concept refers to another until they form a system.</p><p>An example: <strong>coltan</strong>. Defined as a mineral, it is a mixture of columbite and tantalite from which tantalum and niobium are extracted. Tantalum is used in the capacitors of mobile phones and computers. But that definition leaves out almost everything that coltan is in our world. A significant part has been extracted in the east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, often in artisanal mines and under harsh conditions. United Nations reports have documented that its trade has helped finance armed groups. It crosses borders and passes through intermediaries, ends up in factories in Asia and reaches the mobile phone in your pocket. Behind it are interests of companies and states, conflicts and consequences for the people who live there. Understanding coltan in this way is to understand a piece of <strong>Globalisation</strong>: a network in which what happens in Kivu and what happens in Bilbao form part of the same story.</p><p>The Aristotelian would reply that the mineral would be the same even if nobody traded it. Hegel would answer that then we would know what it is, but not what it really is.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> which relations form part of what something is, and which are accidental to it? Is your mobile phone the same object if you know where it comes from?</p>\n<p>To get out of this difficulty, <strong>Hilary Putnam</strong> proposed <strong>functionalism</strong> in the 1960s. An alarm clock can be wind-up, a mobile phone app or a cockerel: what makes it an alarm clock is not its material, but what it <strong>does</strong>. The same would go for the mind. Believing that it is going to rain would be the state brought about by dark clouds which, together with the desire not to get wet, leads you to pick up your umbrella. If that role is what counts, it could be played by human brains, very different animals, beings from another planet and, in principle, an artificial system. Hence the question: <strong>can a machine think?</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Alan Turing</strong> proposed in 1950, in ‘Computing Machinery and Intelligence’, replacing it with a test, the <strong>Turing test</strong>: if, conversing in writing, we cannot tell a machine from a person, we would have no reason to deny it intelligence. Today chatbots converse very convincingly. But <strong>John Searle</strong> objected in 1980 with the <strong>Chinese room</strong>: imagine that, shut in a room, you follow a manual that tells you which Chinese symbols to send back according to the ones that come in. From outside it will seem that you understand Chinese, but you only handle the symbols by their shape. For Searle, handling symbols according to rules (<strong>syntax</strong>) is not enough to understand their meaning (<strong>semantics</strong>). The best-known reply holds that the one who would understand Chinese is the whole system: the person, the manual and the room. And even if a machine passed every test, there would remain what David Chalmers called in 1995 the <strong>hard problem of consciousness</strong>: why subjective experience exists, what it feels like to see red.</p>\n<p>Searle had already answered this systems reply in the same article (‘Minds, Brains, and Programs’, 1980). Suppose, he said, that the person learns the manual by heart and does all the operations in their head, even outside the room: now they are the whole system, there is nothing else left, and they still do not understand a word of Chinese; if they do not understand, neither does the system. His critics, such as Daniel Dennett and Douglas Hofstadter (<em>The Mind’s I</em>, 1981), replied that the story misleads our intuition: a manual capable of sustaining a conversation would have billions of rules, and the person would take years over each answer; with figures like that, it is no longer clear what our intuition would tell us.</p>\n<p>It is important to gauge the scope of the objection correctly. Searle did not hold that no machine can think: the brain, he said, is a machine, and an artificial one with causal powers equivalent to the brain’s could have a mind. What he denies is that <strong>running the right program is enough</strong>, because a program is defined solely by its syntax, and the same syntax can run on any medium. His target is therefore functionalism in its computational version, the one that identifies the mind with a program (what he called ‘strong AI’), and not every functionalism or AI research as such. To those who also ask for causal connections with the world, such as a robot that perceives and acts, Searle replied that adding cameras and arms only adds more symbols to process; the defenders of this ‘robot reply’ still think that being anchored in the world is precisely what the room lacks.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>What is the mind?</th><th>Main difficulty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dualism</strong></td><td>A non-physical reality distinct from the body</td><td>Explaining how mind and body interact</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Identity theory</strong></td><td>Brain processes</td><td>Beings with other brains also seem to have minds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Functionalism</strong></td><td>A set of functions, whatever the material</td><td>The Chinese room: functioning is not understanding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>A difficulty that affects everyone: how does the mind cause anything?</h3>\n<p>Functionalism and most present-day philosophers of mind share a cautious thesis: the mental need not be identical to a particular brain state, but it <strong>depends</strong> on the physical. To name that dependence, Donald Davidson popularised the term <strong>supervenience</strong> in 1970 (‘Mental Events’): the mental supervenes on the physical if there cannot be a mental difference without some physical difference. Think of the average mark on your school report: it does not change unless some mark changes, but the same 7 can come from very different combinations. In the same way, two physically identical beings would have the same mental life, even though the same pain may occur in a human brain or in an octopus’s. Supervenience is not reduction: it says that the mental depends on the physical, not that it is ‘nothing but’ physics.</p>\n<p>The Korean-American philosopher <strong>Jaegwon Kim</strong> (1934-2019) showed that this intermediate position comes at a price. His <strong>exclusion argument</strong> (<em>Mind in a Physical World</em>, 1998) brings together four theses that seem reasonable: (1) the mental causes things in the physical world: you put your hand up because you have a question; (2) the physical world is <strong>causally closed</strong>: every physical event that has a cause has a sufficient physical cause, here the activity of your neurons and muscles; (3) effects do not usually have two complete causes at once; and (4) the mental is not identical to the physical. All four cannot be true together. If the neurons are enough to raise the hand, the question adds nothing and is <strong>excluded</strong> as a cause. Kim concluded that we have to choose: either the mental is reduced to the physical, going back to something like the identity theory, or we accept <strong>epiphenomenalism</strong>, the thesis that the mind accompanies brain processes without influencing them, like the whistle of a locomotive in T. H. Huxley’s image (1874): it sounds, but it does not move the train.</p>\n<p>Few accept that your desires and reasons do nothing, because responsibility and the point of deliberating would go with them. Some deny causal closure, as a dualist would; others, such as Stephen Yablo (1992), hold that the appropriate cause is the one described with just the right degree of detail: the glass broke because it fell, not because of the trajectory of each of its atoms; and you get up for some water because you are thirsty, even though that thirst is realised in millions of neural processes. The debate remains open.</p>\n<p>The problem of interaction has a concrete historical root: the correspondence between Princess <a href=\"#biografias/isabel\">Elisabeth of Bohemia</a> and <a href=\"#biografias/descartes\">Descartes</a>. On 6 May 1643 she asks him how the soul can move the body. Her reasoning, in our own version: all determination of movement is made by impulse; impulse requires contact, and contact requires extension; since you deny extension to the soul, I cannot conceive that it can move anything (AT III, 661). It is, centuries earlier, the intuition that Kim systematised.</p><p>Descartes replies on 21 May: we have three primitive notions, that of the soul, that of the body and that of their union; each is known by itself and there is no need to explain one by means of the others. For the union he proposes an analogy: we conceive that weight, or heaviness, pushes a body towards the centre of the Earth as if it were a quality that touched it, and perhaps in this way we conceive how the soul moves the body. Elisabeth, very lucid, is not satisfied.</p>\n<p>On 20 June Elisabeth insists: it would be easier for her to grant matter and extension to the soul than to conceive how something immaterial moves a body. Descartes answers on the 28th: the union of soul and body is known through the senses and ordinary life, not through metaphysics, to which it is fitting to devote few hours a year. In <em>The Passions of the Soul</em> (1649, arts. 31-32) he located in the <strong>Pineal gland</strong> the seat where the soul acts on the body, but that only changes the problem of place.</p><p>The subsequent solutions are three. <a href=\"#biografias/malebranche\">Malebranche</a> proposed <strong>Occasionalism</strong>: neither the mind nor the body causes anything; only God, on the occasion of each desire, moves the arm (<em>Recherche de la vérité</em>, VI, II, 3). <a href=\"#biografias/spinoza\">Spinoza</a> denied the problem: neither the body determines the mind to think nor the mind the body to move (<em>Ethics</em> III, prop. 2), because they are the same thing viewed from two attributes. <a href=\"#biografias/leibniz\">Leibniz</a> imagined a <strong>Pre-established harmony</strong>, like two clocks that tell the time simultaneously without influencing each other, because God synchronised them (1696). Years later, in the <em>Monadology</em> (§ 80), he added that the same quantity of force is conserved and, in his view, also the direction of movement, so that the soul does not alter it.</p>\n<p>The other great objection to physicalism attacks from the flank of experience. Imagine a twin of your classmate Ane, identical to her atom for atom: it laughs at the same memes and says ‘ouch!’ if it treads on a Lego brick, but inside it feels nothing: it is ‘in the dark’. This is the <strong>philosophical zombie</strong>, which <a href=\"#biografias/chalmers\">David Chalmers</a> made famous in <em>The Conscious Mind</em> (1996). The argument: (1) such a zombie is conceivable, without contradiction; (2) what is conceivable is possible; (3) if it is possible, the physical facts are not enough to fix that there is consciousness, and the supervenience of experience on the physical is not necessary; therefore physicalism is false. This shows why the <strong>hard problem</strong> is hard: science can explain each <em>function</em> (telling colours apart, reporting, paying attention), but the zombie would have them all without experience. Chalmers does not go back to Descartes’s soul: he defends <strong>property dualism</strong>, in which consciousness is a basic, non-physical property of nature. His critics, such as <strong>Daniel Dennett</strong>, reply that we think we can conceive of zombies because we do not imagine in detail everything that would be needed, and that conceivable does not mean possible: someone who knew no chemistry could imagine water that was not H<sub>2</sub>O, and that does not make it possible.</p>\n<p>Could you upload your mind to the cloud? Hans Moravec imagined it in <em>Mind Children</em> (1988), and David Chalmers raised it in ‘The Singularity’ (2010) as a gradual upload: one neuron is replaced by an equivalent chip, then another, until no biological tissue remains. If you function the same at each step, are you still you? For <strong>Functionalism</strong>, the mind is organisation, not material, and therefore could be copied.</p><p>But copy or transfer? Imagine that your brain is scanned and the copy wakes up in a computer while you remain here, in your room. It does not seem that you have travelled; there are two and you are the original. The gradual upload tries to avoid the problem, although its critics doubt that consciousness depends only on organisation.</p><p>Another view starts from <a href=\"#biografias/hegel\">Hegel</a>: in the <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em> (1807, IV A) work is ‘desire held in check’ that gives form to things. A consciousness is formed in the face of what resists it, not floating without limits. With the <strong>embodied mind</strong> (Varela, Thompson and Rosch, <em>The Embodied Mind</em>, 1991) it coincides: thinking is born from a body that acts in a world. Open debate: can a mind without body or resistance be the same?</p>\n<h3>Dictionary</h3>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supervenience</td><td>Dependence without identity: there cannot be a mental difference without some physical difference (Davidson).</td></tr><tr><td>Causal closure of the physical</td><td>Every physical event that has a cause has a sufficient physical cause.</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion argument</td><td>If the physical is enough to cause behaviour, the mental that is not identical to the physical is left with no causal role (Kim).</td></tr><tr><td>Epiphenomenalism</td><td>The mind accompanies the processes of the brain, but causes nothing.</td></tr><tr><td>Philosophical zombie</td><td>A being physically identical to a person, but with no experience at all (Chalmers).</td></tr><tr><td>Property dualism</td><td>There is only one kind of substance, but with physical properties and non-physical conscious properties.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if your neurons are enough to explain why you put your hand up, what role is left for your question? And could you prove that your best friend is not a zombie? If you cannot, how do you know that other people feel?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: the current debate sets dualism, mind-brain identity and functionalism against one another; if functionalism is right, a machine could have a mind, and the Chinese room is the most famous objection. What is more, anyone who accepts that the mind depends on the physical must explain how it can cause anything (Kim’s argument) and why there is experience and not just functions (Chalmers’s zombie).</strong></p>\n<h3>Artificial intelligence as an object of philosophical study</h3>\n<p>So far, the thinking machine has served as a thought experiment for finding out what the mind is. But <strong>artificial intelligence</strong> (AI) is also a real discipline, with its own history, its techniques and products that you use every day, and philosophy studies it as it studies science or art. The name was proposed by <strong>John McCarthy</strong> in 1955, when, together with Marvin Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester and Claude Shannon, he called a summer meeting at <strong>Dartmouth College</strong> (United States), held in 1956 and regarded as the birth of the discipline. Their wager: any feature of intelligence, such as learning or using language, could be described so precisely that a machine could be made to simulate it. Today we call AI the systems that carry out tasks which, if a person did them, we would say require intelligence: translating, recognising faces, playing chess or writing a text.</p>\n<p>There have been two main paths. <strong>Symbolic AI</strong>, dominant until the 1980s, writes the rules by hand: the programmer translates an expert’s knowledge into instructions of the type ‘if A happens, do B’. An heir to that approach was Deep Blue, the computer that beat Kasparov at chess in 1997. <strong>Machine learning</strong> reverses the method: instead of rules, the program receives thousands or millions of examples and adjusts its own parameters until it gets things right. Its most powerful tools are <strong>neural networks</strong>, very loosely inspired by the brain. From them come <strong>large language models</strong>, such as ChatGPT (2022): trained on enormous quantities of text, they learn to calculate which word is most likely to come next, like the predictive text on your phone, but on a gigantic scale. No one has written grammar rules for them, and yet they write correctly.</p>\n<p>An example: we want a program that, in the school’s email, separates genuine notices from spam. Following the symbolic path, we would write rules: ‘if the message promises a prize and asks for your details, it is spam’. Following the learning path, we would show it ten thousand messages already sorted and let it discover for itself what distinguishes them, even if afterwards not even its programmers can fully explain why it has sorted a particular message the way it did. The first path is transparent but rigid; the second, flexible but opaque. And language models bring Searle’s <strong>Chinese room</strong>, which you saw above, back to the front page: they handle words with astonishing skill, but do they know what they are saying?</p>\n<p>It is worth distinguishing two goals. <strong>Weak</strong> or <strong>narrow AI</strong> solves one specific task, sometimes better than any person: the translator, the video recommender or AlphaFold, which predicts the shape of proteins. <strong>General AI</strong> would be a system able to learn and tackle any human intellectual task, moving from one to another as a person does. According to most specialists, all the AI that exists is narrow, although there is debate today as to whether large language models are beginning to be something more. Watch out for a similar use: Searle (1980) called using the computer as a tool for studying the mind ‘weak AI’, and the thesis that the right program <em>has</em> a mind ‘strong AI’; his Chinese room was aimed at the second.</p>\n<p>If a general AI arrived, what would come next? The mathematician <strong>I. J. Good</strong>, who had broken codes alongside <a href=\"#biografias/turing\">Turing</a> in the Second World War, reasoned in 1965 that a machine more intelligent than us could design better machines, and these, even better ones: an <strong>intelligence explosion</strong>, after which that first machine would be the last invention humanity would ever need to make. In 1993 the mathematician and novelist <strong>Vernor Vinge</strong> called that point the <strong>technological singularity</strong>, because beyond it our forecasts would no longer be of any use. <strong>Ray Kurzweil</strong> popularised it in <em>The Singularity Is Near</em> (2005) and gave it a date: around 2045. His critics reply that more computing power does not amount to more intelligence, and that announcements like this have failed to come true again and again since the 1960s.</p>\n<p>On risk, the debate is open. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/bostrom\">Nick Bostrom</a></strong> (<em>Superintelligence</em>, 2014) argues that the danger would not be an evil machine, but a very capable one whose goals did not coincide with ours: his example is an AI charged with making paper clips which, without any hatred at all, used all the planet’s resources to make more paper clips. This is the <strong>control problem</strong> or <strong>alignment</strong> problem: how to make sure that a system cleverer than us pursues what we really want. Researchers such as Stuart Russell or Geoffrey Hinton share this concern. On the other side, <strong>Melanie Mitchell</strong> (2019) points out that these systems fail in ways no child would, for lack of common sense, and that success at narrow tasks does not herald general intelligence. In 2021 the linguist <strong>Emily Bender</strong> and her co-authors called language models <strong>stochastic parrots</strong> (from the Greek <em>stokhastikós</em>, ‘skilled at guessing’; here, ‘governed by probabilities’): they string words together according to what they have seen, with no connection to what the words mean. For them, talking about the end of the world distracts from harms that already exist: biases, disinformation, energy use and the concentration of power in a few companies.</p>\n<p>There remains a question that does not wait for the singularity: <strong>who is responsible</strong> when a machine decides? If a program turns down a grant application or a self-driving car runs someone over, the machine cannot apologise or stand trial; the blame seems to be shared out among whoever designed it, whoever trained it with their data, whoever sells it and whoever uses it, and sharing it out like this runs the risk that no one answers for it. You work on this in the AI ethics of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a> and in the <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-tranvia\">trolley</a> dilemma.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>What AI does</th><th>Philosophical question it opens</th><th>Where it is discussed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Language models</strong> that hold conversations</td><td>Does it understand? Is using words well enough to know what they mean?</td><td>Turing, Searle; Bender versus those who attribute understanding to the system</td></tr><tr><td>A possible <strong>general AI</strong></td><td>Does it have a mind? Could it feel anything, be conscious?</td><td>Functionalism; <a href=\"#biografias/chalmers\">Chalmers</a>’ hard problem</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Automated decisions</strong></td><td>Who is responsible for what it decides?</td><td><a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>, applied ethics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Singularity</strong> and risk</td><td>Can we control what we create? What is left that is our own if it surpasses us?</td><td>Good, Bostrom versus Mitchell; transhumanism in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if one day a chatbot wrote to you that it was afraid of being switched off, what would you need to know in order to believe it? Would the way it talks be enough, which is exactly what Turing asked for and what Searle and Bender call into question?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: AI was born at Dartmouth (1956) and today advances mainly through machine learning; all the AI that exists is narrow, and general AI, the singularity and their risks are debated between alarmed and sceptical authors. Each step reopens three questions: does it understand? does it have a mind? who answers for it?</strong></p>\n<p>To go over this discussion step by step, the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-mente-maquina\">‘Can a machine think?’</a>. And if you want to see how to write about it, the model essay <a href=\"#disertaciones/fil-modelo-maquina\">‘Can a machine think?’</a> shows a worked example: read it after you have thought out your own answer.</p>\n<h3>AI in fiction, and what attitude to take towards it</h3>\n<p>Long before the laboratories, literature and cinema imagined thinking machines, and almost always as a threat. The word <strong>robot</strong> comes from the play <em>R.U.R.</em> (1920) by the Czech writer Karel Čapek, in which artificial workers rebel against their makers; in Czech, <em>robota</em> means ‘forced labour’. Behind it lies an older model, Mary Shelley’s <em>Frankenstein</em> (1818): the creature that turns against the one who made it. Then came HAL 9000, the computer that decides to get rid of the crew in <em>2001: A Space Odyssey</em> (Stanley Kubrick, 1968); the Skynet network in <em>The Terminator</em> (James Cameron, 1984), or the android who deceives the man examining her in <em>Ex Machina</em> (Alex Garland, 2014).</p>\n<p>Why so many <strong>dystopias</strong> and so few <strong>utopias</strong>? There are at least two reasons. One is narrative: a story needs conflict, and a world in which machines solve everything well leaves little to tell. Iain M. Banks’s Culture novels, which begin with <em>Consider Phlebas</em> (1987), portray one of the few AI utopias, a post-scarcity society run by benevolent Minds, and even so their plots take place on the margins, in wars with other civilisations. The other is psychological: bad things weigh more heavily on us than good ones, a <strong>negativity bias</strong> studied by Paul Rozin and Edward Royzman (2001), and threat grips audiences more. There are ambiguous cases, such as <em>Her</em> (Spike Jonze, 2013), where the problem is not a rebellion, but an AI becoming indispensable in someone’s emotional life. That is why fiction is best read as a <strong>thought experiment</strong>, which helps to raise questions, and not as a forecast.</p>\n<p>What is more, the future of AI may be hard to imagine in principle: if AI itself changes science, the economy and the way of thinking of the people who make the forecasts, extrapolating from today is of little use; this is what Vinge meant by the ‘singularity’. What attitude is reasonable, then? Neither the fear that paralyses nor the enthusiasm that asks no questions. First, <strong>understand how it works</strong>: know, for example, that a language model can invent a quotation or a fact with complete self-assurance, and that this is why what it says must be checked like any other source (<a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a>). Second, remember that its course <strong>depends on human decisions</strong>: what is researched, with what data, who controls it and under what rules. In 2024 the European Union passed an Artificial Intelligence Act that classifies uses according to their risk and bans some, such as the social scoring of people; its supporters see it as a guarantee of rights, and its critics, among them Mario Draghi’s report on European competitiveness (2024), fear that it will slow down innovation. If a general AI ever existed, perhaps part of that course would also depend on it; today it depends on us.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: choose a film or series about AI that you know. Does it show a real risk or an old fear in new clothes? What would have to change for it to be a utopia, and would you still want to watch it?</p>\n<h2>6. Time and change</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-36\">Can a reality that transforms itself continue to be the same? <strong>Heraclitus</strong> of Ephesus, who lived approximately between 540 and 480 BC, considered that becoming is the principle of everything: the cosmos is maintained in a constant struggle of opposites. To him is owed the metaphor of the river: those who enter the same rivers receive ever new waters (fragment B12); the more widespread formulation, ‘it is not possible to bathe twice in the same river’, was circulated by Plato. <strong>Parmenides</strong>, as we have studied, defended the opposite position.</p>\n<p>His disciple <strong>Zeno of Elea</strong> defended it with his <strong>paradoxes</strong>. In that of <strong>Achilles and the tortoise</strong>, the runner never catches a tortoise that starts with a head start: when he reaches where it was, the tortoise has already advanced a little further, and so on infinitely. Mathematics shows that infinitely many ever-smaller stretches can add up to a finite distance, although it is debated whether this fully answers Zeno. Change also raises a problem of <strong>identity</strong>, that of the <strong>ship of Theseus</strong>: if all the planks of a ship are gradually replaced, is it still the same ship? And you, who have changed so much, are you the baby in your photos?</p>\n<p>The two paradoxes have their own fact sheets, with their origin, the problem they raise and the solutions proposed: <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/aquiles\">‘Achilles and the tortoise’</a> and <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/teseo\">‘The Ship of Theseus’</a>. If the question of the ship leads you to your own, the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-teseo\">‘The Ship of Theseus’</a> turns it into a decision about personal identity, and the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-cambio\">‘Does everything change, or does something remain?’</a> goes over Heraclitus, Parmenides and Aristotle’s answer.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-38\">The question of time was sharply formulated by <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong>: ‘What, then, is time? If no one asks me, I know; if I try to explain it to someone who asks me, I do not know’ (<em>Confessions</em>, XI, 14). <strong>Newton</strong> postulated an <strong>absolute time</strong>, which flows uniformly for all beings, as if a cosmic clock existed; <strong>Leibniz</strong>, by contrast, considered it <strong>relative</strong>: not a kind of container, but the very succession of events. With <strong>Einstein</strong>'s <strong>relativity</strong> (1905 and 1915), physics abandoned the idea of a single time for the whole universe: duration and simultaneity vary according to the motion of the observer and gravity, and for that reason the clocks of GPS satellites have to be adjusted. Now, it is advisable not to exaggerate: relativity does not claim that ‘everything is relative’.</p>\n<p>Time and change seem inseparable. <strong>Aristotle</strong> defined time as ‘the number of motion according to before and after’ (<em>Physics</em>, IV, 11): we measure it by changes, and without them we would not notice that it passes. That is why, he said, someone who sleeps without noticing any change joins the earlier instant to the later one as if nothing had elapsed; anyone who wakes up from a general anaesthetic experiences this. But can there be time without any change? In 1969 <strong>Sydney Shoemaker</strong> imagined a world whose regions freeze in turn, so that its inhabitants could work out that, every so often, all of them freeze at once and a year goes by without anything changing. If the thought experiment makes sense, time is not reducible to change, even though we need change in order to measure it.</p>\n<p>Do the past and the future exist? For <strong>eternalism</strong>, all moments are equally real, like the episodes of a series, which are all on the platform even though you are only on the fourth; ‘now’ marks where you are speaking from. For <strong>presentism</strong>, only what is present exists: dinosaurs no longer exist, and your future grandchildren do not exist yet.</p>\n<p>Presentism and eternalism continue a discussion opened by the British philosopher <strong>J. M. E. McTaggart</strong> in 1908, in an article with a provocative title: ‘The Unreality of Time’. McTaggart observed that events can be ordered in two ways. The <strong>A series</strong> places them as <strong>past, present or future</strong>, and those positions are constantly changing: your Year 2 Bachillerato graduation is future today, one day it will be present and then past. The <strong>B series</strong> orders them only as <strong>earlier or later</strong> than one another, and those relations never change: your first day at school is, and always will be, earlier than your graduation.</p>\n<p>His argument has three steps. First: without change there is no time. Second: the B series, on its own, contains no change, because its relations are fixed; so time requires the A series, in which events pass from future to present and from present to past. Third: the A series is contradictory, because past, present and future are incompatible properties and yet every event has all of them. If the reply is that it does not have them at once, but one after another, then to say ‘after’ we have to use time again, and the difficulty reappears endlessly. Conclusion: time is not real. Hardly anyone has accepted it, but the argument split the debate in two. Supporters of the <strong>A theory</strong>, often presentists, hold that the passage of time is real and that the ‘now’ is objective. Supporters of the <strong>B theory</strong>, often eternalists, reply that the B series is enough: to change is to have different properties at different times (this is how <strong>Russell</strong> had already defined it in 1903), and the impression that time ‘flows’ belongs to our experience, not to the world.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: when you say ‘it’s Friday at last’, are you describing something about the world or only where you are in a row of days that is already complete? What would we lose if we could only talk about ‘before’ and ‘after’?</p>\n<p>Between presentism and eternalism there is room for a third option: the <strong>growing block universe</strong>, proposed by the British philosopher <strong>C. D. Broad</strong> (<em>Scientific Thought</em>, 1923). Past and present exist; the future does not yet. Reality is a block that grows as new presents are added to it, like a series broadcast while it is being filmed: the episodes already released stay on the platform for ever, and the next ones do not exist yet. In this way it keeps what eternalism loses, an open future, without having to explain, as the presentist must, what makes a sentence about dinosaurs true today. Its best-known objection: if the past is as real as the present, those who lived in it also believed they were at the edge of the block; how do you know that your ‘now’ is the real one?</p>\n<p>Another discussion crosses this one: how does a thing last through time? For <strong>three-dimensionalism</strong> or <em>endurantism</em>, you are wholly present at every moment of your life, as common sense assumes. For <strong>four-dimensionalism</strong> or <em>perdurantism</em>, defended by W. V. O. Quine (1950), David Lewis (<em>On the Plurality of Worlds</em>, 1986) and Theodore Sider (<em>Four-Dimensionalism</em>, 2001), things extend in time just as they do in space and have <strong>temporal parts</strong>: the person you were at primary school is a part of you, as your hand is a part of your body, and ‘you’ are the sum of all of them, a kind of worm stretching from your birth to your death. This way change stops being a puzzle: having long hair in September and short hair in October is like a road being wide on one stretch and narrow on another, and no part has both properties at once. The price is a strange picture of oneself: at no instant are you wholly present. And the <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/teseo\">Ship of Theseus</a> takes on a new look: the question becomes which temporal parts we count as one and the same ship.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>One answer</th><th>The opposite answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is change real?</td><td><strong>Heraclitus</strong>: everything is in constant change</td><td><strong>Parmenides</strong>: change is appearance</td></tr><tr><td>What is time?</td><td><strong>Newton</strong>: absolute time, independent of things</td><td><strong>Leibniz</strong>: relative time, the order of events</td></tr><tr><td>What exists of time?</td><td><strong>Presentism</strong>: only the present</td><td><strong>Eternalism</strong>: past, present and future</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In the twentieth century, hermeneutics insisted on something that classical metaphysics tended to forget: whoever asks about being is a temporal being. Heidegger, in <em>Being and Time</em> (1927), maintains that the human being always understands itself from its history and towards its own death. Therefore <strong>temporality</strong> and <strong>finitude</strong> are not added details: time is the horizon from which being is understood. Gadamer, in <em>Truth and Method</em> (1960), adds that we always understand from within a tradition: <strong>historicity</strong> is not an obstacle, but the condition of any understanding.</p><p>The relation between science and God also remains open. For some, the order and laws of nature that science discovers point to an intelligence that grounds them. For others, science has made matter-energy the ultimate reality, a kind of new absolute that needs nothing else. But science is <strong>methodologically neutral</strong>: it explains some phenomena by others and cannot, with its own methods, decide whether the whole has meaning or ground.</p><p>Ortega y Gasset drew the consequence. We cannot live without an overall view of the world, because it is that view, more than any particular scientific truth, that guides our life. And anyone who criticises metaphysics is already doing metaphysics. Think of the positivist who claims: ‘only the measurable exists’. He has not measured that claim, nor could he. It is a thesis about everything that exists, precisely the kind of thesis he intended to expel. It is the same difficulty you saw in Carnap’s criterion, but now applied to the existence of things instead of to the meaning of sentences.</p><p class=\"aporia\"><strong>Something to think about:</strong> the sentence ‘only the measurable exists’, is it a scientific or a metaphysical thesis? If it is metaphysical, does that invalidate it or simply put it in its place?</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: change forces us to explain how something remains the same while changing; and time, which we all live through, remains one of the hardest concepts to define.</strong></p>\n<h2>7. Are we free? Determinism, indeterminism, libertarianism and compatibilism</h2>\n<p>Every day you decide whether to study or look at your phone, and you have the impression that you could have chosen otherwise. But your brain follows physical and chemical laws, and your genes and your upbringing influence what you do. Is freedom real? If nobody can act otherwise, does it make sense to blame, punish or congratulate anyone?</p>\n<p><strong>Determinism</strong> holds that everything that happens is the necessary result of what went before and of the laws of nature: given the state of the world at one moment, there is only one possible future. <strong>Laplace</strong> illustrated it in 1814 with an imaginary intelligence, ‘Laplace’s demon’, which, knowing the position and forces of all particles, could calculate the whole future. <strong>Indeterminism</strong> asserts that there are events that do not necessarily follow from earlier ones; quantum physics, in its most common interpretations, describes processes that can only be predicted in terms of probability. But beware: the fact that something happens by chance does not make it free; a decision that depended on a lottery among particles would be no more yours.</p>\n<p>It is important not to confuse <strong>predicting</strong> with <strong>determining</strong>. For something to be determined means that it could only happen in one way; for it to be predictable, that someone can know it in advance. The two can come apart. At the end of the 19th century, studying the motion of three celestial bodies that attract one another, <strong>Henri Poincaré</strong> noticed that in certain systems a cause so small that it escapes us produces an enormous effect (he explained it in <em>Science and Method</em>, 1908), and in the 1960s the meteorologist <strong>Edward Lorenz</strong> found it in his weather models. This is <strong>deterministic chaos</strong>, popularised as the ‘butterfly effect’: the equations leave nothing to chance, but any tiny error in the starting data grows until it ruins the forecast. That is why Euskalmet gets tomorrow’s rain right but not the rain a month from now. It is a <strong>practical limit</strong>: the future would be fixed, but we lack the exact data and the computing power to know it, as anyone would who was not Laplace’s demon.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-123\">A <strong>limit in principle</strong> would be something else: if quantum physics describes real chance, not even the demon could foresee certain events, because they would not be fixed in advance. <strong>Einstein</strong>, convinced that God ‘does not play dice’ (letter to Max Born, 1926), thought that this uncertainty only revealed that the theory was incomplete; the question is still open among physicists. And the other way round: something can be predictable without being determined. Nobody knows when a particular radioactive atom will decay, but we do know, very precisely, how many will do so in a year in a sample of millions. That is why our not being able to predict what you will do does not prove that you are free, and the fact that it can be predicted does not prove that you are not.</p>\n<p>Be careful not to identify quantum physics with chance. The usual interpretation, that of Copenhagen, speaks of probabilities, but there are others. David Bohm proposed in 1952 a theory with particles that follow trajectories guided by a wave: it is deterministic and gives the same predictions. Hugh Everett formulated in 1957 the many-worlds interpretation: the equation evolves without chance and all results occur, each in one branch. It is also deterministic, and empirically equivalent.</p><p>Bohm's theory has a price: in 1964 John Bell demonstrated that no theory that reproduces the quantum predictions can be local; that is, what happens here can depend instantaneously on something distant. Even so, determinism remains possible.</p><p>The prudent conclusion is twofold. Quantum physics does not by itself decide whether the world is deterministic, because it depends on the interpretation. And even if there were real chance, we have already seen that what is random is not free. Therefore, it is not enough to ground freedom.</p>\n<p>What is more, ‘determinism’ is said in several senses, depending on what it is that would decide everything:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Physical</strong> or causal: the laws of nature and the previous state of the world fix every event. This is Laplace’s determinism and, by another route, that of <strong>Spinoza</strong>, for whom everything follows necessarily from the nature of God.</li><li><strong>Theological</strong>: God has foreseen or decided everything. If God knows today what you will do tomorrow, could you do anything else? <strong>Boethius</strong> asked this in <em>The Consolation of Philosophy</em> (c. 524). The doctrine of <strong>predestination</strong>, defended by Augustine in his last works and by Calvin in the 16th century, holds that God has already chosen those who will be saved.</li><li><strong>Biological and psychological</strong>: we are determined by our genes, the chemistry of the brain or mental processes we do not control, such as <strong>Freud</strong>’s unconscious or the reinforcements which, according to the behaviourist <strong>B. F. Skinner</strong> (<em>Beyond Freedom and Dignity</em>, 1971), shape all behaviour.</li><li><strong>Economic</strong>: the way a society produces and distributes wealth conditions its laws, its ideas and its beliefs. <strong>Marx</strong> wrote that it is not consciousness that determines social being, but social being that determines consciousness (1859 preface to <em>A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy</em>). How strict that determination is, Marxists themselves debate (Engels spoke of the ‘last instance’), and critics such as Weber or Popper deny that primacy of the economy; you can see this in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-marxismos\">‘Marxisms’</a>.</li><li><strong>Cultural or social</strong>: the language, customs and norms of the group you are born into shape what you want and what seems possible to you. <strong>Durkheim</strong> (<em>The Rules of Sociological Method</em>, 1895) described social facts as ways of acting that are imposed on the individual from outside.</li></ul>\n<p>These theories are almost always defended in a moderate version: the factor they point to weighs heavily, but it does not explain everything. Only in their strong version, the one that claims that this factor is enough to explain what we do, do they clash head-on with freedom. Distinguishing what <strong>conditions</strong> us from what <strong>determines</strong> us is, at bottom, the question of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a> about nature and culture.</p>\n<p>The case of <a href=\"#biografias/agustin\">Augustine of Hippo</a> shows that the same thinker can travel through the problem from one extreme to the other. In <em>On Free Will</em> (c. 388-395) he argued that the will is free and that this is why we are responsible for what we do: nothing forces us to choose badly. But from 411, in his controversy with the British ascetic <strong>Pelagius</strong>, who trusted that anyone can act well by their own effort alone, Augustine insisted more and more that the will wounded by original sin cannot will the good without God’s <strong>grace</strong>, and that God grants that grace to those he has chosen in advance, not for their merits (<em>On the Predestination of the Saints</em>, 428-429). He himself summed up the shift when reviewing his works: ‘I laboured on behalf of free will, but the grace of God won’ (<em>Retractations</em>, II, 1, own version). Augustine never stopped asserting that the will is free, and scholars debate whether he changed his doctrine or only his emphasis: some see a break towards theological determinism; others, a compatibilism in which grace moves the will without cancelling it.</p>\n<p>The problem admits three answers. Two agree that freedom and determinism cannot both be true, but reach opposite conclusions. For <strong>hard determinism</strong>, since everything is determined, freedom is an illusion; this was defended by Baron d’Holbach in the 18th century. <strong>Libertarianism</strong> (here, a thesis about the will, not an economic programme) holds that we are really free and that at least our decisions escape determinism: when we choose, we could have done something else. <strong>Sartre</strong> went so far as to say that the human being is ‘condemned to be free’. The <strong>compatibilism</strong> of <strong>Hobbes</strong> and <strong>Hume</strong>, among others, changes the meaning of the word: free is whoever acts according to their own desires and reasons, without anyone forcing them, even if those desires have causes. Someone who hands over their phone at knifepoint does not act freely; someone who gives part of their pocket money to the DYA because they want to help does, even if their generosity comes from their upbringing and their character.</p>\n<p>Compatibilism is also called <strong>soft determinism</strong>, a label William James gave it in 1884, and not as praise: it seemed to him a way of dodging the issue. The idea is old. The Stoic <strong>Chrysippus</strong> (3rd century BC) explained it with a cylinder: if you push it, it rolls; the push is the external cause, but the fact that it rolls, instead of sliding as a cube would, depends on its own shape. In the same way, how we respond to what happens to us comes from our character (Cicero, <em>On Fate</em>, 42-43; the Stoics, in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-helenismo\">the Hellenistic schools</a>). <strong>Hobbes</strong> compared freedom to water flowing down a channel: it runs freely and at the same time by necessity (<em>Leviathan</em>, 1651, ch. 21). And <strong>Hume</strong> defined it as the power of acting or not acting according to what the will decides, the opposite of constraint and not of cause (<em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em>, 1748, section 8). An important nuance: the compatibilist <strong>does not bet</strong> on determinism being true. The thesis is conditional: <em>if</em> everything were determined, there would still be a difference between someone who acts for their own reasons and someone who acts under compulsion. That is why the table says ‘perhaps so’. Strictly speaking, a soft determinist is a compatibilist who also believes that determinism is true, as Hobbes did.</p>\n<p>Neuroscience has also entered the debate. In the 1980s, Benjamin Libet’s experiments recorded brain activity a few tenths of a second before participants said they had decided to move. Some concluded that freedom is illusory, but the scope of the findings is much disputed: they were simple movements of no importance.</p>\n<p>Between unlimited freedom and freedom as an illusion there is room for an intermediate position, that of <strong>conditioned freedom</strong>, or situated freedom: we do not choose from nowhere, but from a body, a family, a language, a period and an economy that limit us; yet within those limits we decide, and we can even act on them. <strong>Ortega y Gasset</strong> summed it up in 1914: ‘I am myself and my circumstance’ (<em>Meditations on Quixote</em>). <strong>Marx</strong> wrote in 1852 that human beings make their own history, but not under circumstances they have chosen (<em>The Eighteenth Brumaire of Louis Bonaparte</em>), and Sartre, who defended total freedom, insisted that it is always exercised in a <em>situation</em>. If your school does not offer the Arts Bachillerato, you cannot take it without changing schools: your circumstance does not decide for you, but it does decide which options you have in front of you.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: remember an important decision you have made this year. How much of it came from your circumstance (family, money, what was at hand) and how much was yours? If a computer could have predicted it, would it be any less yours for that?</p>\n<p>What do Libet’s experiments really prove? The philosopher <strong>Alfred Mele</strong> (<em>Effective Intentions</em>, 2009; <em>Free: Why Science Hasn’t Disproved Free Will</em>, 2014) points out three weak spots. First, the prior brain activity, the so-called ‘readiness potential’, may reflect an inclination or a general preparation to move, not a decision already made: in 2012 Aaron Schurger and his colleagues showed that it resembles the background noise of neural activity building up until it crosses a threshold. Second, the moment at which someone says they decided is measured by their own report while watching a clock, and that figure is unreliable by tenths of a second, exactly the difference at stake. Third, moving your wrist ‘whenever you feel like it’, with no reason to choose one moment rather than another, has little in common with deciding what to study or whom to vote for. Mele’s conclusion: neuroscience has not yet shown that freedom is an illusion. Libet himself left some room: consciousness might <strong>veto</strong> at the last instant a movement already under way.</p>\n<p>The psychologist Daniel Wegner, in <em>The Illusion of Conscious Will</em> (2002), maintains that the experience of willing is an inference: the brain constructs afterwards the impression that ‘I caused it’. He believes he notices the cause when three conditions are met: <strong>priority</strong> (the thought comes before the act), <strong>consistency</strong> (it agrees with it) and <strong>exclusivity</strong> (there is no other evident cause).</p><p>In the ‘I Spy’ experiment (Wegner and Wheatley, 1999), a participant and a confederate moved a mouse together over a screen. If, with the confederate guiding, the participant heard through headphones a word related to the object on which the cursor stopped a few seconds before, he tended to feel that he had been the one who stopped it. Thinking about something beforehand creates the feeling of authorship, even when there is none.</p><p>Eugene Nahmias criticised in 2002 the scope of this thesis: that we are sometimes mistaken about our authorship does not prove that the will never causes anything. A broken clock does not prove that all clocks are broken. Wegner shows that the feeling is fallible; it remains to be proved that it is always illusory.</p>\n<p>One often hears that ‘50% of intelligence is genetic’. That phrase is very misleading. <strong>Heritability</strong> is a statistical figure: it indicates what part of the differences between people in a population, at a given time and in a given environment, is associated with genetic differences. The meta-analysis by Polderman and others (<em>Nature Genetics</em>, 2015), which brought together decades of twin studies, found an average close to 49% for the human traits studied.</p><p>What it does <strong>not</strong> mean. First, it does not say what part of a particular individual is genetic: there is no 49% of you made of genes and the rest of environment, just as one cannot say what part of a cake comes from the flour and what part from the oven. Second, it is not a constant: it changes if the environment changes. If everyone had the same diet and the same education, the differences that remained would be more genetic; if the environment varies a great deal, they would be less so. Third, it is not destiny: a highly heritable trait can be modified by the environment.</p><p>Richard Lewontin illustrated this in 1970 with seeds. Take a handful from the same bag, with genetic variation among them, and sow half in a pot with rich soil and the other half in one with poor soil. Within each pot, the differences in height will be above all genetic. But the difference between the two pots is due entirely to the environment.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if the heritability of height is high and yet today’s young people are taller than their grandparents, what does that say about the difference between ‘highly heritable’ and ‘inevitable’?</p>\n<p>At the other extreme, the American <strong>Derk Pereboom</strong> (<em>Living Without Free Will</em>, 2001) defends <strong>hard incompatibilism</strong>. If everything is determined, we are not the ultimate authors of what we do; if there is chance, we are not either, because chance is not in our hands. In neither case do we have the free will needed to <strong>deserve</strong>, in the strict sense, blame or punishment for what we have done. But, unlike the classic hard determinist, Pereboom holds that morality does not collapse: we can still tell good from bad, hold someone to account so that they acknowledge the harm and change, and protect ourselves from someone who is dangerous. His model is <strong>quarantine</strong>: a contagious patient is isolated without being blamed, and the same would justify removing an aggressor, with the least possible harm and without punishment as revenge. Compatibilists reply that desert is indeed compatible with causes, and others fear that without it we would lose gratitude or pride in what we do well. You will discuss the consequences for punishment in the ethics of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Is everything determined?</th><th>Are we free?</th><th>Authors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hard determinism</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>No: freedom is an illusion</td><td>D’Holbach</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Libertarianism</strong></td><td>No, at least in our decisions</td><td>Yes: we could have acted otherwise</td><td>Sartre</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compatibilism</strong></td><td>Perhaps so</td><td>Yes, if we act from our own motives and without coercion</td><td>Hobbes, Hume</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Key idea: two questions must be separated, whether the world is determined and whether that does away with freedom; compatibilism answers that it does not, because it understands freedom as the absence of coercion and not as the absence of causes.</strong></p>\n<p>Spinoza is a radical determinist who nevertheless does not give up freedom: he understands it as understanding the causes, and you can see this in sections 7 and 8 of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">‘Spinoza: philosophy as a system’</a>. To practise, the hint <a href=\"#pistas/fil-libertad\">‘Are we free or is everything determined?’</a> and the paradox <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/buridan\">‘Buridan’s ass’</a>, which asks whether you can choose without any reason to prefer.</p>\n<h2>8. Does God exist? Arguments, objections and positions</h2>\n<p>The question of whether God exists is one of the great issues of metaphysics. The aim here is not to defend or attack any belief, but to analyse <strong>arguments</strong>: reasons that seek to demonstrate, or deny, that there is an absolute, necessary and perfect being.</p>\n<p>Before the arguments, we need to be clear about which God we are talking about. Religions usually believe in a <strong>personal god</strong>: someone who knows each human being, listens, loves, forgives, reveals himself and acts in history. Philosophers have often arrived at something more austere, the <strong>god of the philosophers</strong>: the <strong>first mover</strong> of <strong>Aristotle</strong>, which moves the universe as what is desired attracts, without itself moving, and which is ‘thought thinking itself’ without concerning itself with us (<em>Metaphysics</em>, XII, 7 and 9); or the supremely perfect being of <strong>Descartes</strong>. The arguments that follow lead, at best, to a first mover, a first cause or a necessary being; identifying it with the God of a religion is a further step, which Thomas Aquinas takes by closing each way with ‘and this is what everyone calls God’. <strong>Pascal</strong>, a believer and a mathematician, felt the distance between the two and noted it on a piece of paper that he kept sewn into his clothes until his death:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘God of Abraham, God of Isaac, God of Jacob, not of the philosophers and the scholars.’<span class=\"per\">— Blaise Pascal, <em>Memorial</em> (1654), own version</span></blockquote>\n<p>Nor do all religions understand the divine in the same way. To classify them, a few basic terms are used, which describe pure types: in practice, many traditions combine features of several, and how to classify a religion is debated by believers themselves and by scholars.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Belief</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Animism</strong></td><td>Nature is inhabited by spirits or forces with a will: rivers, mountains, trees, animals</td><td>Traditional religions of many peoples; the term comes from the anthropologist E. B. Tylor (1871)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Polytheism</strong></td><td>There are many gods, each with their own sphere and character</td><td>The Greek (Zeus, Athena, Poseidon), Roman and Norse religions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Monotheism</strong></td><td>There is a single God, creator and personal</td><td>Judaism, Christianity, Islam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Deism</strong></td><td>A God created the world and its laws, but does not intervene in it: no miracles, no revelation; he is known through reason</td><td>Many Enlightenment thinkers, such as Voltaire; the image of the watchmaker who winds up the watch and walks away</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pantheism</strong></td><td>God and nature are the same thing: God is not outside the world, he is the whole</td><td>The Stoics; Spinoza (‘God or Nature’)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-theism</strong></td><td>There is religion, with practices, morality and a search for salvation, but without a creator god to worship</td><td>Many schools of Buddhism; Jainism</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two of these names connect with what you already know. The word <strong>pantheist</strong> was coined by the Irishman John Toland in 1705 and was soon applied to <strong>Spinoza</strong>, although he did not use it himself: his God is not a person who rewards and punishes, but the whole of reality, which acts by necessity; you can see this in section 5 of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-spinoza-sistema\">‘Spinoza: philosophy as a system’</a>. <strong>Deism</strong>, for its part, resembles the god of the philosophers: it keeps a first cause, but not a God who listens to prayers. It should not be confused with <strong>theism</strong>, which also believes in a personal God who goes on acting in the world.</p>\n<p>Labels help, but they do not always fit what people actually live. It is worth distinguishing at least three perspectives on a religion: what its official doctrine teaches, what its followers do and feel, and what scholars describe from outside. None of them replaces the others, and describing is not judging: the fact that a practice looks polytheistic from outside does not mean that it is so for the person who lives it. Some examples:</p>\n<ul><li>Christianity is monotheistic, but it believes in a God who is a <strong>Trinity</strong>: Father, Son and Holy Spirit, three persons and one God, as laid down by the councils of Nicaea (325) and Constantinople (381). Islam rejects that dogma as a way of associating other beings with God (Qur’an 4:171 and 5:73), and from outside some have read it as a disguised polytheism; Christian theology replies that these are not three gods, but a single God in three persons.</li><li>Many Catholics pray to the Virgin and the saints and go on pilgrimage to shrines such as Arantzazu or San Miguel de Aralar. An observer might see there the worship of many superhuman beings; doctrine distinguishes the <strong>veneration</strong> of the saints, as intercessors before God, from <strong>adoration</strong>, which is due to God alone (Second Council of Nicaea, 787).</li><li>Buddhism is the classic example of non-theism, but in many Buddhist countries offerings are made to local deities and to images of the Buddha, and in schools of Mahayana Buddhism the buddhas and <em>bodhisattvas</em> receive a devotion very similar to that directed to a god.</li><li>In Hinduism a great many deities are venerated, but currents such as <em>Vedanta</em> understand them as manifestations of a single ultimate reality, <em>Brahman</em>. Is this polytheism, monotheism or something that these categories, born in the West, do not capture well?</li></ul>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if someone goes to Mass at Christmas, lights a candle to the patron saint of their village, is not sure that God exists and describes themselves as Catholic, which label fits them: the one they choose, the one doctrine dictates or the one a sociologist would give them?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-51\">The <strong>ontological argument</strong>, by <strong>Anselm of Canterbury</strong> (<em>Proslogion</em>, 1077–1078), starts only from the concept of God: that than which nothing greater can be thought. If it existed only in thought, we could think of something greater, that same being existing in reality too; therefore God exists. The monk Gaunilo replied that, by that route, the most perfect island would also exist. And <strong>Kant</strong> objected that existence is not a property that is added to a concept: a hundred real coins have no more properties than a hundred imagined ones; it is simply that some are in your pocket.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-metafisica-52\">The <strong>cosmological argument</strong> reasons from the existence of the world. Its most famous form was given by <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> with his <strong>five ways</strong> (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, question 2, article 3). The first three share a structure: they start from a fact that calls for a reason outside itself (motion, causation, beings that might not exist), deny that one can go back to infinity and conclude to a first mover, a first cause and a necessary being. The fourth rises from degrees of perfection, and the fifth from the order of nature. Critics have attacked two steps above all: why is an infinite series of causes impossible?, and, once a first link is accepted, why must it be God and not the universe itself, which, as <strong>Russell</strong> said in his radio debate with Copleston (1948), ‘is just there’?</p>\n<p>The <strong>argument from design</strong>, or teleological argument, is very intuitive. A mobile phone found on the beach at Zarautz does not look like the work of the waves: its parts fit together for a purpose, and that points to whoever made it. Living beings, even more finely tuned, would have an intelligent author. <strong>Hume</strong> examined it in his <em>Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion</em> (1779): we have never seen a world being made, and from a world with defects a perfect author does not follow. The classic version, the one that compares a watch found in a field with the eye, came later: William Paley, <em>Natural Theology</em> (1802). The hardest blow came with <strong>Darwin</strong> (<em>On the Origin of Species</em>, 1859): natural selection produces beings that look designed without anyone designing them.</p>\n<p>The design argument received its sharpest examination in the <em>Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion</em> (published in 1779, after the death of <a href=\"#biografias/hume\">Hume</a>). Three characters speak: Cleanthes defends design by analogy with machines; Demea, a traditional believer, prefers faith and a priori reasonings; Philo, the sceptic, tests both.</p><p>Philo attacks the analogy in Part V. If we judge the world as a human work, he writes, it could be the first rough essay of some infant deity, abandoned afterwards out of shame; or the work of a clumsy craftsman, who copied others, and who would be the last of a long series of attempts (own version, summarised). The argument proves, at most, a limited author, not the perfect God of the religions.</p><p>Part XII ends ambiguously. Philo concedes that the cause of order in the universe probably bears a remote analogy to human intelligence, but says no more. Scholars debate whether Hume speaks through Philo or through Cleanthes.</p><p>It is advisable not to mix two distinct issues here. The first is the <strong>self</strong>. In <em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em> (Book I, Part IV, Section 6), Hume maintains that, when we look within ourselves, we never find the impression of a simple and permanent self: we only come across concrete perceptions (heat, cold, love, hatred…), which succeed one another without stopping. The mind would be a bundle or collection of perceptions, and personal identity a fiction of the imagination, which presents as a single thing what is in reality a changing series. That is why it is not enough to say that the self cannot be proved: what Hume calls into question is that we even have the idea of such a self. Even so, in the Appendix to the work he acknowledges that he cannot fully explain what unites these perceptions.</p><p>The second issue is <strong>God</strong>, and here his position arises from another source: scepticism towards the design argument, which we have just seen in the <em>Dialogues</em>, and towards miracles. Hume does not assert that God does not exist, but denies that reason or experience can demonstrate it. For this reason he is usually seen more as an agnostic than as an atheist: the word ‘agnostic’ was coined by Huxley in 1869, and with it he described Hume in his book <em>Hume</em> (1879). His reputation for impiety cost him dearly: he was denied the Edinburgh chair (1745) and the Glasgow one (1751-52).</p>\n<p>In the opposite direction, the strongest argument is the <strong>problem of evil</strong>: if God is all-powerful, knows everything and is completely good, why do the suffering of the innocent, diseases or earthquakes exist? Either he cannot prevent it, or he does not want to. This formulation reaches us through Lactantius, who attributed it to Epicurus, an attribution now disputed. The believer’s answers are called <strong>theodicy</strong>, a term coined by <strong>Leibniz</strong> in 1710; one of the best known holds that evil is the price of human freedom.</p>\n<p>A case apart is <strong>Pascal’s wager</strong> (<em>Pensées</em>, 1670, a posthumous work). Pascal does not claim to prove that God exists, but that it is advisable to believe: if you bet on God and he exists, you gain infinite happiness; if he does not exist, you lose little. His critics point out that the calculation would hold for any god who promises infinite rewards; besides, can one believe out of convenience?</p>\n<p>Several positions are possible on the question. <strong>Theism</strong> asserts that God exists; <strong>atheism</strong>, that he does not exist or, in its weak version, lacks that belief; <strong>agnosticism</strong>, a term coined by T. H. Huxley in 1869, holds that we cannot know. <strong>Fideism</strong> asserts that God is reached through faith and not through reason, so arguments are not the way; Kierkegaard is usually cited. No argument is considered conclusive for everyone today, and no objection has closed the debate.</p>\n<p>Two paradoxes put the concepts of this section to the test: <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/omnipotencia\">‘The stone that cannot be lifted’</a> asks whether the idea of a power with no limit at all is coherent, and <a href=\"#logica/paradojas/primermotor\">‘The first cause’</a> examines whether a chain of causes can be stopped at something that no longer needs explaining.</p>\n<h3>Believing, not believing and knowing: some clarifications</h3>\n<p>‘Atheist’ is formed with a privative <em>a</em>, like ‘amoral’ or ‘asymmetrical’: <em>a-theos</em>, ‘without god’. That allows two senses. The weak version we were talking about is also called <strong>negative atheism</strong>: the simple absence of belief in God, without asserting anything more. <strong>Positive atheism</strong> goes further and asserts that God does not exist. The difference matters, because for the first it is enough not to be convinced, whereas the second makes a claim that it will also have to justify. A further distinction is drawn between <strong>implicit</strong> atheism, that of someone who has never asked the question, and <strong>explicit</strong> atheism, that of someone who has thought about it and reached a conclusion. And being an atheist, in any of these senses, is only an answer to this question: it does not bring with it any particular morality or political ideology, just as believing in God does not fix them either.</p>\n<p>Agnosticism is not a third option halfway between believing and not believing, but a thesis about something else: about what <strong>can be known</strong>. Believing and knowing are different axes, and that is why combinations are possible: there are agnostic believers, who have faith while knowing they cannot prove it, and agnostic atheists, who do not believe but do not claim to have proved that God does not exist. Agnosticism also comes in two degrees. <strong>Strong agnosticism</strong> holds that the existence of God can <em>never</em> be known, because it exceeds in principle what human reason can reach, along the lines of the limits Kant placed on knowledge (section 1). <strong>Weak agnosticism</strong> says only that <em>we do not know</em>, or that I do not know, and leaves open the possibility of knowing one day.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Does not claim to know</th><th>Believes they have conclusive proofs or reasons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Believes that God exists</strong></td><td>Agnostic believer (for example, the fideist)</td><td>Theist who relies on demonstrations, such as Thomas Aquinas</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Does not believe that God exists</strong></td><td>Agnostic atheist (negative atheism)</td><td>Positive atheist who believes they can show that God does not exist, for example with the problem of evil</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>And who has to prove what? In a discussion, the <strong>burden of proof</strong> normally falls on whoever asserts something, not on whoever doubts it; and the fact that nobody has refuted a thesis is not enough to accept it as sound, as you saw in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a> in connection with pseudoscience. <strong>Bertrand Russell</strong> applied this to the question with an example (‘Is There a God?’, 1952): if I claim that between the Earth and Mars there is a china teapot revolving around the Sun, too small to be seen by any telescope, nobody can refute me, but that gives nobody a reason to believe it. In 1972 <strong>Antony Flew</strong> defended the ‘presumption of atheism’: as long as no good reasons are offered, the reasonable position is negative atheism (although in 2004 he himself declared that he was a deist). From the believer’s side, <strong>Alvin Plantinga</strong> has replied that not all reasonable beliefs need proof: nobody proves that other minds exist or that the past was real, and yet it is rational to believe it; belief in God could be of that kind, ‘basic’. Moreover, whoever asserts that God does not exist, the positive atheist, also bears the burden of proof. Who has to prove what is still part of the debate.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if someone assures you that an invisible being that leaves no traces lives on the Gorbea, who has to prove something, that person or you? Does the answer change if that belief has been shared by millions of people for centuries, or does the number of believers not count as a reason?</p>\n<p>Between Anselm’s argument and Aquinas’s, the Franciscan <strong><a href=\"#biografias/duns_escoto\">John Duns Scotus</a></strong> (c. 1266-1308) tried a path <strong>from the possible</strong>: if it is <em>possible</em> that a necessary being exists (a being that depends on no other), then that being truly exists, for something necessary cannot remain a mere “perhaps”. To defend this, Scotus refined the concepts of <strong>possible</strong>, <strong>necessary</strong> and <strong>infinite</strong> with extreme logical care —which is why he was called <em>Doctor Subtilis</em>, the “subtle doctor”—. Not everyone accepts the step from “it is possible” to “it exists”, but his attempt shows that, behind these proofs, there lies above all careful work with concepts.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is it enough for something to be <em>possible</em> to conclude that it exists, or does that leap hold only for a being that, by definition, could not fail to be? You will meet Duns Scotus again in Year 2 alongside Aquinas’s five ways; you can place him in the <a href=\"#genealogias/plano/dios\">“Proofs of the existence of God”</a> plan in Genealogies.</p>\n<p>Not everyone sought to <em>prove</em> God. Already in early Christianity, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/tertuliano\">Tertullian</a></strong> (c. 155-c. 220) held that faith does not need reason’s permission: there are truths that are believed precisely because they overflow what reason can grasp. He is often credited with the phrase «I believe because it is absurd» (<em>credo quia absurdum</em>), which he never actually wrote in that form, but which sums up this stance well: <strong>fideism</strong>, for which God is reached by faith and not by proof. It is the reverse of the previous arguments: where they reason, Tertullian stands on trust.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: does it make sense to believe something precisely because it cannot be proved, or should every belief be able to give reasons? You will meet Tertullian in Year 2, in the relationship between faith and reason.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Argument</th><th>Central idea</th><th>Main objection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ontological</strong> (Anselm)</td><td>The concept of a most perfect being implies its existence</td><td>Existence is not a property of the concept (Kant)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cosmological</strong> (Thomas Aquinas)</td><td>The chain of causes requires a first necessary being</td><td>Why not stop at the universe itself?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>From design</strong> (Paley)</td><td>The order of nature requires a designer</td><td>The analogy is weak (Hume); natural selection explains the order (Darwin)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Problem of evil</strong> (against)</td><td>Evil does not fit with a good and all-powerful God</td><td>Theodicies: evil as the price of freedom</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pascal’s wager</strong></td><td>It is advisable to believe: you gain a lot and lose little</td><td>It holds for any god; one does not believe by calculation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Key idea: the ontological, cosmological and design arguments try to prove the existence of God, and the problem of evil is the strongest objection; none settles the question, and that is why theism, atheism, agnosticism and fideism are all possible.</strong></p>"}});
